  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 1208 AM MDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nSatellite imagery shows a few patchy low clouds over the eastern\nColorado plains and a quiet night ahead. Lows tonight will be in \nthe low 50s at low elevations and 30s-40s for the mountains. \nTemperatures Tuesday afternoon warm up slightly but remain right \nat seasonal averages, with highs in the low 80s for the Denver \nmetro. A shift to SW flow aloft will result in the return of \nmonsoonal moisture for the mountains. As such, a few isolated \nshowers and storms may develop over the high terrain late in the \nafternoon. The chance for showers over the Denver metro and east \nColorado plains will remain very low (<20%). However, high-res \nmodel soundings do generally show inverted-V profiles which would \nindicate some potential for gusty winds (up to 35 mph) should any \nhigh-based/virga showers develop.\n\nDeterministic and ensemble global model solutions are in fairly \ngood agreement that a mid-level disturbance will track north from \nNew Mexico to Colorado on Wednesday. Just to the east, a ridge \nwill continue to build over the Great Plains with an anticyclone \ncentered over Oklahoma, which will encourage steady S-SE flow at \nthe low levels. The bulk of the moisture is expected to be \nstationed over New Mexico and southern Colorado. However, PWs in \nthe 1.0-1.3\" range will still be advected over the much of our CWA\neast of the foothills. Such anomalous moisture this late in the \nseason will be something to watch as a slight west or north shift \nin this setup would push the flash flooding threat into our area. \nAt this time, it looks like the better chance for localized heavy \nrainfall in our area will remain over the east Colorado plains. \nFor the Denver metro and I-25 corridor, Wednesday will likely \nstill bring the best chance for scattered showers although the \ntiming remains a bit uncertain. If dense cloud cover develops \nearly in the day, this will limit instability considerably and \nlead to very isolated shower coverage. Regardless of cloud cover, \nthere is a decent chance (55%) for at least a round or two of \nshowers in the overnight hours as the right- entrance region of a \njet streak provides additional forcing. West of the foothills, \nmonsoonal moisture will be closer to seasonal values but still \nabove normal, allowing for afternoon showers and storms to develop\nwith daytime destabilization.\n\nMoisture at the low elevations will peak around Thursday morning \nand this is likely to limit instability for the day due to a low \nto mid level cloud deck and temperatures struggling to climb above\nthe upper 60s. Such limited instability will decrease shower \ncoverage Thursday afternoon although scattered showers are still \nlikely over the high terrain and eastern Colorado plains.\n\nA weak shortwave will track through Colorado Friday afternoon and \npromote scattered showers and storms, mainly over the high terrain.\nEnsemble guidance is in good agreement that Saturday and Sunday \nwill be drier days with mostly sunny skies and temperatures \nrunning a few degrees warmer than normal. Uncertainty enters the \npicture early next week with some global model solutions \nsuggesting an upper level trough or cut-off low, and others \nshowing a return to ridging. Long-range ensembles do hint at a \nmore amplified wave trend for next week.",
